   "answer_head": "The core idea across your books is that **weight on screen is a property of timing and spacing, not of drawing accuracy** \u2014 a cannonball drawn beautifully will still float unless its motion is timed to suggest mass [S5]. Each object has its own weight, construction, and flexibility, so it behaves individually when a force acts on it; that behavior\u2014the combination of position and timing\u2014is the real basis of animation [S4]. In practice this means heavy objects must be given plenty of time to start, stop, or change direction, and the animator must apply plenty of force to make them do so convincingly [S1].\n\n**What the timing rules are**\n\n- **Inertia and acceleration:** Things don't jump from rest to full speed; even a cannonball accelerates to maximum when fired, and a car hitting a wall keeps moving through the impact while folding up [S4]. The visible interval between drawings (the spacin"
