   "answer_head": "Your books converge on one clear thesis: **a character reads as heavy not because of how it is drawn, but because of how its motion is timed and spaced** \u2014 weight is a behavior, not an image. And this has both a physical side (Newtonian timing, mass, momentum) and a perceptual/emotional side (the audience reads the movement's purpose, not just its form).\n\n**Core principle \u2014 weight is timing, not drawing.** The way an object behaves on screen, and the effect of weight it gives, depends almost entirely on the spacing of the animation drawings, not on the drawing itself; a beautifully drawn cannonball still won't look heavy if it doesn't behave like one [S5]. This is because a static drawing has no actual weight or forces acting on it \u2014 the animator must supply those through motion [S4]. Concretely, heavy objects need *plenty of time* to start, stop, or change direction, and the animator mu"
