   "answer_head": "Editors and directors build suspense without dialogue by controlling what the audience knows and when they know it, then using cutting, composition, and motion to convert that knowledge into dread. The core trick is information asymmetry \u2014 let the viewer understand more than the on-screen character, and the gap itself becomes tension [S11][S4]. Hitchcock's framing makes this literal: low-angle shots share a character's limited awareness, while high-angle shots reveal the danger they don't see, and combining them is \"the true nature of suspense\" \u2014 his bomb-under-the-table example, where we know what's coming and wait [S3].\n\n**Editing mechanics** are the strongest tool. Cutting back and forth at an increasing pace creates suspense on its own, especially the classic rush-to-rescue intercut [S5]. The same technique appears in the _Dunkirk_ example \u2014 we hear the enemy plane, see bombs walk to"
  },
