   "answer_head": "Suspense without dialogue is built on one core principle: the audience knows something the character doesn't, and the film gives them time to imagine the worst. Everything else \u2014 editing, camera, pacing, sound \u2014 is a mechanism for controlling that gap between what we know and what we fear.\n\n**Information control is the foundation.** Hitchcock's rule holds: there can be no real suspense unless the audience knows what is going to happen; the bomb under the table only works if we know it's there [S3]. This is the \"limited vs. open information\" strategy \u2014 when Anne approaches the car in the classic example, we know a bomb is planted but she doesn't, so we jump at every click of the car door and worry each mile down the highway [S11]. Suspense is what's suspended: emotionally intense waiting, the audience held between curiosity and fear, needing enough information to anticipate the bad thing "
  },
