   "answer_head": "Lighting and color are the fastest shorthand an audience reads before they interpret a single line of dialogue. They work at a \"gut, emotional level\" that reaches viewers on one plane while their conscious brain processes the story on another [S7]. The same physical scene lit bright and warm reads differently than if the background behind the actors is \"dark and murky\" \u2014 the viewer may suspect someone is lying, sad, or there is a sinister subtext [S2]. Because the background is \"part of the same picture, and thus part of the overall message,\" how it's lit is almost as important as how the acting area is lit [S4].\n\n**Why it works: color is an international, transcultural language.** The sources repeatedly stress that color carries instinctive, almost linguistic meaning: bluish light signals night worldwide, warm golden reads as dawn, orangey as sunset [S4]; we say people \"look green\" when"
  }
